  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1121 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nOur trend of above normal temperatures (10-15 degrees), continues as \ntoday's high temperatures will be in the upper 60s and lower 70s for \nthe plains. More cloud cover is expected as a shortwave tracks north \nof Colorado today and into the Great Plains Wednesday. This \nshortwave also allows the push of a weak front into our region this \nafternoon/evening increasing wind gusts up to 20-30 mph along the \narea of the Cheyenne Ridge. Elevated fire weather conditons will be \npossible, but relative humidity values are expected to stay above \ncritical thresholds. The passage of this weak front will cause \nWednesday's high temperatures to be slightly lower in the low to mid \n60s.\n\nQuiet weather will continue through the rest of the work week as the \nupper level ridge axis dominates our area through Friday. High \ntemperatures will be in the mid to upper 60s, but a few solutions \nfavor reaching or exceeding 70F. At this time, the chances of \ntemperatures exceeding 70F are about 20-40% for Thursday and Friday. \n\nFor the advertised system over the weekend, models appear to be \ntrending more bullish on the rain and snow potential for both the\nmountains and the plains. Generally, a low pressure system moves \ninto SW Colorado and Northern New Mexico Saturday evening before \nlee- cyclogenesis produces a potential surface low near far \nsoutheastern Colorado. This will give us our next chance for snow \nshowers for the higher terrain and rain for the plains. Intensity \nand placement of this low/trough will likely determine \naccumulation totals. Cooler temperatures are also expected with \nhighs on the weekend in the upper 50s and lower 60s. Monday should\nbe a pleasant fall day before our next storm system moves in \naround mid-week.",
